+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Running ${PKGSTEM} on OpenBSD
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ledger hardware wallets
=======================

Members of the following group can access supported Ledger devices. Create
the group if needed, or verify that the existing group is dedicated to
Ledger access.

    # groupadd ${AGAVE_LEDGER_GROUP}

Add your login account to the group.

    $ doas usermod -G ${AGAVE_LEDGER_GROUP} "$(id -un)"

Log out and back in for the new group membership to take effect.

Configure both /etc/hotplug/attach and /etc/hotplug/detach. For each
missing script, install ${TRUEPREFIX}/share/examples/agave/ledger-hook
under that name, owned by root:wheel with mode 0755. In an existing
script, add the following line before any exit or change to the arguments.

    ${TRUEPREFIX}/libexec/agave/ledger-access "${0##*/}" "$1" "$2"

Enable and start hotplugd(8).

    # rcctl enable hotplugd
    # rcctl start hotplugd

Reconnect the Ledger, unlock it, and open the Solana app. Read its public
key from your regular account to verify access.

    $ solana-keygen pubkey usb://ledger

To set permissions on existing uhid(4) nodes at boot, add
${TRUEPREFIX}/libexec/agave/ledger-rescan to /etc/rc.local.

Local test validator
====================

A 2 GiB soft data size limit was sufficient to start solana-test-validator
on OpenBSD. If startup fails with an allocation error, check the limit with
ulimit -Sd in ksh(1). If it is lower, try raising it in the same shell
before starting the validator again.

    $ ulimit -Sd 2097152

If ulimit -Hd shows a hard limit below 2097152 KiB, ask the administrator
to raise datasize-max for your login class (see login.conf(5)), then log
out and back in.

The RPC, WebSocket, and faucet services listen on all IPv4 interfaces,
even with --bind-address 127.0.0.1. For local testing, use pf(4) to
restrict inbound access to their ports (TCP 8899, 8900, and 9900 by
default).
